WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.760
Part 4. You will hear part of an ecology lecture about invasive species of plants and animals.

00:00:09.440 --> 00:00:14.800
First, you have some time to look at questions 31 to 40.

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:10.320
Now listen carefully and answer questions 31 to 40.

00:01:13.760 --> 00:01:21.440
Today I'm going to talk about invasive species. Let me start by saying what an invasive species

00:01:21.440 --> 00:01:28.720
is and what it's not. Invasive species are any animal or plant that is introduced into

00:01:28.720 --> 00:01:34.000
an environment by humans and which is then harmful to that environment.

00:01:35.199 --> 00:01:40.080
It's important to be clear that not all introduced species are invasive.

00:01:41.120 --> 00:01:47.919
Many introduced or non-native species thrive in new areas without posing any threat.

00:01:49.279 --> 00:01:55.360
In some cases, invasive species have changed the natural world beyond recognition,

00:01:56.080 --> 00:01:59.599
so let's look at the different ways they can be problematic.

00:02:00.959 --> 00:02:08.639
First of all, invasive species may eat native species or sometimes they may bring a disease

00:02:08.639 --> 00:02:15.679
with them which native species have never faced before and therefore have no defences against.

00:02:16.960 --> 00:02:23.839
Often the invasive species breed very quickly, which further adds to the problem of native

00:02:23.839 --> 00:02:30.320
species losing their sources of food. Species invasions are one of the biggest

00:02:30.320 --> 00:02:35.759
causes of damage in an ecosystem, actually putting its survival at risk.

00:02:37.759 --> 00:02:45.520
So how do invasive species spread? Without a doubt, the biggest cause is human activity.

00:02:46.479 --> 00:02:53.199
This could be intentional or it could be accidental, such as when people who've been

00:02:53.199 --> 00:03:00.160
on holiday in another country come back with, say, the seeds of plants on their clothes or shoes.

00:03:01.520 --> 00:03:09.759
Plants and animals, especially insects, arrive in or on the cargo of ships and then escape into

00:03:09.839 --> 00:03:18.000
their new home. But sometimes humans deliberately move animals and plants around the world,

00:03:18.800 --> 00:03:27.679
for example, to use them to control pests on farms or to be pets. This can go very wrong

00:03:27.679 --> 00:03:34.080
if those animals and plants move into wild settings and start breeding or begin growing

00:03:34.160 --> 00:03:40.639
in ways that weren't predicted. Let's now look at an example of an

00:03:40.639 --> 00:03:48.800
invasive species here in Australia. Rinella marina is a species of toad that was deliberately

00:03:48.800 --> 00:03:58.399
introduced from Hawaii in 1935 as a form of biological control. It was hoped that the toads

00:03:58.399 --> 00:04:04.320
would eat the grey-backed beetles responsible for destroying crops of sugar on many of the

00:04:04.320 --> 00:04:12.320
plantations. At first, just a handful of toads were released by scientists into Queensland,

00:04:13.039 --> 00:04:23.119
but this number soon grew as other states followed suit. Within two years, 62,000 young toads had

00:04:23.119 --> 00:04:30.399
been released into the wild. The toads did nothing to protect the plantations, but they

00:04:30.399 --> 00:04:36.079
did reproduce rapidly and could soon be found all over the northern half of the country.

00:04:37.600 --> 00:04:44.640
The toads are poisonous at every stage of their life cycle, and anything that eats them will die.

00:04:45.519 --> 00:04:54.079
My second example regarding invasive species is the United Kingdom. Actually, there are more than

00:04:54.079 --> 00:05:03.600
3,000 invasive species there, including some that are extremely common. Some invasive plants,

00:05:03.600 --> 00:05:09.600
such as Japanese knotweed, have had a devastating impact on parts of the UK.

00:05:10.079 --> 00:05:16.959
Gardeners in the 19th century considered it a beautiful ornamental plant, which it is when

00:05:16.959 --> 00:05:24.239
it's kept under control. But it soon spread into the countryside and remains a problem even to

00:05:24.239 --> 00:05:32.640
this day as it's so hard to eradicate. Another invasive plant is rhododendrons,

00:05:33.040 --> 00:05:40.000
which can be found in UK parks and woodlands. Their introduction dates back to 1763,

00:05:41.040 --> 00:05:47.200
but they're now seen as harmful because they block out so much light that native wildflowers

00:05:47.200 --> 00:05:54.959
can't grow beneath them. And then there are grey squirrels, which are one invasive species almost

00:05:54.959 --> 00:06:02.399
everyone in the UK will have come across. But they're not the only invasive species

00:06:02.720 --> 00:06:09.760
that we've come across. They were brought to the UK from North America and introduced to private

00:06:09.760 --> 00:06:17.760
estates around the 1870s, but are now found everywhere, from forests to city squares.

00:06:19.359 --> 00:06:26.799
Grey squirrels have outcompeted the smaller, native red squirrels. They both eat the same food,

00:06:27.200 --> 00:06:30.799
carry a type of virus that is deadly to the red squirrels.

00:06:32.239 --> 00:06:38.880
Red squirrel populations have collapsed, and there are only a handful of sites left in the UK

00:06:38.880 --> 00:06:44.480
where they're found. An important question for ecologists worldwide is,

00:06:45.359 --> 00:06:49.440
what can we do to tackle the problem of invasive species?

00:06:50.320 --> 00:06:56.399
The first step in controlling invasive species is learning about the behaviour of new species

00:06:56.399 --> 00:07:03.200
coming into the country. Monitoring is an important part of this, so that we can know

00:07:03.200 --> 00:07:08.000
if the new species begins to have a negative impact in its new environment.

00:07:09.519 --> 00:07:15.519
One effective way to keep track of invasive species is to create a database for the whole

00:07:16.160 --> 00:07:23.920
country. That way, all relevant authorities and agencies can share important information

00:07:23.920 --> 00:07:31.519
and take whatever actions needed. But the public also have a vital role to play in this process.

00:07:32.559 --> 00:07:39.119
They should be encouraged to photograph harmful species, because this helps with identification,

00:07:40.079 --> 00:07:47.679
and then to report when and where these were observed. But it's important to tell people

00:07:47.679 --> 00:07:55.359
not to destroy or even touch what they've found. Now, I'm going to move on to an example...

00:07:58.880 --> 00:08:06.079
That is the end of part four. You now have one minute to check your answers to part four.

